  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts today’s medicinal chemistry workflow with a proposed future workflow centered on a tighter design–make–test cycle. In the middle, a circular loop links computational molecular design, chemical synthesis, and biological testing, with arrows showing repeated iteration and a final output of “novel bioactives.” This core graphic suggests that discovery improves when these stages feed directly into one another rather than operating separately.\n\nThe left side summarizes the current situation as limited and fragmented: a narrow reaction toolkit, emphasis on sp2-rich chemical space, manual expert-driven decisions, poor integration between stages, and progress focused on only one compound series at a time. Together, these points imply a slower, more constrained exploration of molecules.\n\nThe right side presents the future workflow as broader and more automated. It highlights a more powerful synthetic toolkit, access to more three-dimensional chemical space, autonomous molecular design, close integration of all stages, and the ability to pursue multiple compound series in parallel. Overall, the figure communicates a shift from a linear, human-led, chemically restricted process to an integrated, iterative, and higher-throughput platform for discovering new bioactive compounds.",
